WEBVTT

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.740
Chip Huyen:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:05.760
Hey there.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.170
So, I am Chip.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.830
Thank you so much for
coming to my talk.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.590
My career has been mostly
with building AI systems,

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.340
trying to make AI useful
in the real world.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.180
So, I started with NVIDIA; and
then I worked on Snorkel AI,

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.230
which is a data company.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.660
And then, I started another
AI infrastructure startup,

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:25.800
which I sold.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.520
And since then, I'm doing a new
company, which is in robotics.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.940
Anyone here is into robotics?

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.660
One person.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.310
So, yeah. So this is a bunch
of robots that I work with.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:41.610
Like this one, I'm trying to fit
the ring on the robot hands,

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.360
which is weird because they
don't quite have the same width

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.190
as human fingers.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:47.490
But, yeah.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:50.850
So, today the talk is about
software defensibility,

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.130
because that's the type
questions that have been trying

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:57.150
to think about in
the last few years,

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:58.710
because there are two
things that I thought

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.690
of myself as being good at.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.810
One is writing, and
the other is coding.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.970
And I think those are
the top two things

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.880
that AI could easily
automate nowadays.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.170
So, it makes me feel like, okay.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:15.600
So, what should I do that
I can reasonably defend

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.270
in the age of AI?

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.630
So, how many people
here consider yourself

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.120
a software engineer?

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.220
Whoa. A lot of you.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:32.760
How many of you are worried
about AI automating your job?

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:33.660
One person.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.010
Okay. Few more people.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.290
I think this is the kind of
thing you don't say out loud.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:38.340
It's like, yes.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.470
Keep it a secret.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:44.070
Okay. So, one thing I keep
hearing people telling me is

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:48.420
that the cost of building
software is approaching zero.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:49.890
So, I keep hearing
people saying, okay.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.590
It's extremely cheap right
now to generate code, right?

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:55.950
You can just get AI and it can
write you an app, a feature,

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.500
build a website extremely fast.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.960
So, I keep hearing companies
who, in the past,

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.750
maybe they could only
create maybe like 10,

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.000
20 variations for A/B testing.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.321
Now they could generate like

00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:09.000
1.000 variations
for A/B testing,

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.580
because it costs
extremely cheap.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.080
So, it made me
think about, okay.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.380
If the cost of building
software is approaching zero,

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.480
does that mean that the value

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.570
of building software is also
approaching zero, right?

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:24.840
If the cost is zero,
then the value is zero.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.790
So, it makes me
think about, so it's

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.350
like, it made me realize,
it's like, okay.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.120
What should it be
building, right?

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.690
What is the point of
building anything nowadays

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:40.530
if there's no value
associated with it?

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:44.250
So, a few months ago I had
launched a side project.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.330
It's a weekend project.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:46.080
I like it.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.570
It's fun. It got some traction,
300,000 views within a weekend.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:54.750
And then, within a day, somebody
emailed me saying that,

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.520
"I love what you did."

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.310
So, I asked Claude Code
to do exactly that;

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.110
and here's the link
to the website,

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.910
exactly what you just built.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:04.830
So, I felt very
ambivalent about it, right.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:08.160
I was like, on the one
hand, I'm flattered.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.030
Thank you.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.160
But, on the other hand,
what the heck, man?

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:14.910
It's just here's your token,
and I copy and paste it.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:17.160
So, it makes me realize,
it's like any software

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.460
that exists today can easily
be replicated, right?

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.610
If you can ask AI to Airtable,
it can build it.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:26.190
If you ask it to do Asana,
it can build it.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:27.630
And I see there's a
lot of this coming.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.160
I'm actually building
this side project.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.820
It's called kill, kill by
gpt.com where we can vote

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:37.380
on which product is going
to be killed by AI first.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.540
I'm launching it next
week, hopefully.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:43.020
And you guys can use Claude
Code to replicate it.

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:43.350
But, yeah.

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:45.220
So, I'm just curious.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:50.910
Yeah. So, it made me feel
like more excitement,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:53.910
because now I can build
anything that I want.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:58.050
But, at the same time, anyone
can build anything that I want.

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.750
And, of course, not everything
is the same, right?

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:01.950
People talking about, okay.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.530
Not everything can
be easily buildable,

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.800
because you cannot just build
Google in a weekend, right?

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.930
But, at the same
time, I see that --

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:13.290
are you familiar with
METR, like M-E-T-R?

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:17.310
So, they just study the
progress of AI automations.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:19.020
And they do a benchmark.

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.910
Okay. Let's take on the tasks

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:24.420
that usually take,
let's say, an hour.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:28.380
How often that AI can reliably
complete the task, right?

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:33.630
So, it benchmarks on the length
of tasks that AI can complete

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.640
about 50 percent of the time.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:36.660
And you can see this.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:39.030
Okay. In the beginning
it starts very little,

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:40.980
like a few seconds of the task.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:45.210
But now we can reliably complete
tasks that take like 16 hours.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.240
So, it's a very long horizon,
extremely complicated task,

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.160
right, because I feel like,
for a lot of engineers,

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:55.010
I don't think people can
just sit down for 16 hours

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:57.440
and code something
extremely complex.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.450
So, yeah. So, things have
been getting very complex,

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:03.780
so maybe AI cannot build Google
nowadays, given the trajectory.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.100
So, this is log
scale, by the way.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:06.600
But if you do linear
scale, it will look

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:10.950
like very much like
an exponential.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:14.580
So, things like AI can, at
some point, get there.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.820
So, I think it's like the
"Ghibli moment of software."

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.340
So, did anyone use OpenAI

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:24.770
to generate Ghibli picture
style of yourself?

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:26.370
So, in the past, I feel
like there's a trend

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:30.030
of everyone using
image generation

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:33.210
to generate a style Ghibli,
and then I was thinking

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:35.280
that there's no way
Ghibli agrees with this.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:36.960
There's no way.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.520
But, also, as a point, we think

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:39.960
of like a style is
something like,

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:42.720
if you can describe a
style, it's a shorthand

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.730
for the AI to know what to do.

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:46.290
And the artists were
very upset, right,

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.600
because they spent the whole
career developing the style,

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:49.680
and we could just do it.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.540
And I think it's
similar with software.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.820
Anything that exists, just
make it easier for people

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.530
to shorthand and
build it, right.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.420
Maybe if they want to
do something like Asana.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:58.920
It'd take me a lot
harder to describe.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:05:59.820
Okay. Here's the specs.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:00.750
Here's the functionality.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:01.740
Here's the workflow.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.710
But, if it already exists, I can
say, okay, let's just do it.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.720
You know what it is, right?

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.240
So, by the act of you
putting out the software,

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:10.770
you make it easier
for the competitor

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:13.290
to do exactly what he wants.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:16.050
So, the question with AI is
no longer how to build.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.690
Now, if you know what you
want to do, it's very easy.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:22.050
The question is, is like
what you build; and then,

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:25.590
once you build it,
how do you defend it?

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.660
So, I'm curious.

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:32.640
What do you think would be
the moat for your product?

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:35.140
Anyone here is doing a startup?

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:37.590
A few more.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:39.900
So, I'm curious what do you
think would be the moat

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:42.310
for your software startup?

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:46.620
Huh?

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:47.970
Proprietary data.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.440
So, who here
thinks of data as a moat?

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:52.560
A few people.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.870
So, you think that, if you have
a lot of proprietary data,

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:58.020
competitors cannot
compete with you.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.100
Okay. What else?

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:03.120
What else would you consider
like a moat for a company?

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:07.500
Huh? Trust, like branding.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:08.820
Trust, branding.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:11.250
It's another very
interesting question.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.660
Who else? What else?

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:17.970
Customer service.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:18.780
The human layer.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:19.920
So, when you say
customer service,

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:22.660
it's like AI agent
as customer service?

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:28.080
I see. Interesting.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.490
I feel like more and more I
veer toward just not going

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:33.930
through human service.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:35.460
I feel like they can
get away with it.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:37.740
I would probably like to
try something automated.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:39.180
But it's interesting.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:41.040
So, the question of
trust, I think it's

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:42.450
like people have realized,

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:46.890
it's like, trust is not
sufficient because, in the past,

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:49.170
people were a lot more
loyal to your product.

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:52.710
But with AI, because the steps
change, the improvement is

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:55.530
so significant, so you can
see people switch models very

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:56.520
quickly, right?

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.280
When ChatGPT first came out,
everyone thought, okay.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:01.500
There's no way something can
compete with ChatGPT, right?

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:02.670
And then Saturday, right,

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:04.560
Claude Anthropic came
out and said, okay,

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:05.400
now I can do it.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:06.870
And then DeepSeek came
out, and everyone,

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:07.770
a lot would jump to it.

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:09.390
Then DeepSeek turned out
to not work quite well;

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:10.560
will jump to Qwen.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:13.680
So, I think a lot of the
product we have seen

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:19.620
that customer loyalty is hard
to compete with improvement,

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.020
like product improvement.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:24.090
But I think I definitely
agree with you,

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:27.090
just like, building trust is
never going to be a pain.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:31.860
On the question of data,
so data, I used to think

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:33.840
that like, proprietary
data is the most, right,

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:34.980
because you have a lot of data,

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:36.600
then we're going to
compete with you.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:39.720
But then I realized,
given how many companies,

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:43.100
how much money companies are
spending on acquiring data,

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.170
then data is no longer a moat.

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:46.340
Money is a moat, right?

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:48.110
If you have money,
you can just buy it.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:48.890
Have you seen a lot

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:53.750
of AI frontier labs buying
companies just to get the data?

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:54.690
Have you seen that?

00:08:54.690 --> 00:08:56.910
So, if you see, look at
the frontier labs or a list

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:59.430
of their acquiring companies,
some of them acquire companies,

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:02.850
like two or three companies
a week, because the reason is

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:04.950
that, acquire companies so
that they can get data

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:06.210
from that company.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:08.580
So, maybe the competitors
do not have the data,

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:11.760
but maybe a frontier lab can
acquire the competitors' data

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:12.900
and then build something

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:16.020
that works quite well
for the use case.

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:18.020
And it's quite
interesting because,

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:21.500
given how fast people were able
to build a model, a new launch,

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:25.520
a new model, to compete
with existing models.

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:28.640
It turns out that acquiring data
is not the hard part anymore.

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:30.990
And, if it has anything,
it's more like a commodity.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:32.700
So, let's say that you
have a QuickBooks, right?

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:35.700
And you say, QuickBooks will
never sell data to OpenAI.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:38.550
But OpenAI can just go
to my scale AI search,

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:39.780
Micron, and say, Okay.

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:43.200
Now, create a software that
looks exactly like QuickBooks.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.570
Hire like 10,000 accountants

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:47.700
that do all the
workflow you need

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:49.770
as if you were using
QuickBooks, and then use

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:52.110
that data to train the model.

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:54.000
So, people can do
that pretty quickly,

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:59.130
and that's why the oldest data
labeling companies have revenues

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:01.290
of like a billion dollars
because they create a lot

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:04.630
of data extremely,
extremely fast.

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:10.260
Another thing people told me
about is distributions, right,

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:11.790
especially for enterprise.

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:15.330
Let's say they have spent
like months and months trying

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:17.490
to get the software
into the customers.

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:21.120
And they work with, I don't
know, Oracle and like stuff;

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:24.360
I know it's very much
embedded into the ecosystem.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:29.070
It shouldn't be really hard for
people to move away from it.

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:32.070
I think it's, like, just still
a lot of validity in it

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:33.810
but also want to
ask about, okay.

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:36.360
How about for consumer
products, right?

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:40.410
And some things that, like, do
not require heavy integrations.

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:42.570
And a lot was like, oh, I
really do not know, right,

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:44.700
because the consumer product,
like this and that, is,

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:47.730
like, a pretty much easy
issue to replicate.

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:50.190
And also having a distribution

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:53.910
as a moat also makes
a few a bit uneasy,

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:57.000
because the entire
moat strategy depends

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:58.920
on people's annoyance, right?

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:00.900
Something that makes it
extremely hard to use,

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:03.450
so that people cannot
get off your platform.

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:07.110
And also another interesting
model is that I have a friend

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:10.890
who used to be a partner with
a very big firm, like PV firm.

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:14.340
And he also tried to sell to a
lot of manufacturers, right.

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:17.130
And if you go through
compare old school project,

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:18.960
old school approach,
you have to go reach

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:20.790
out to all these
manufacturers, and,

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:22.230
okay, try to sell to them.

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:23.490
And what he did was that, okay.

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:26.880
He just bought a software
company that already has

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:29.460
like 10,000 factories
as a customer.

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:30.090
It was like, okay.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:32.370
These software companies,
they're not AI companies,

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:33.990
it's actually a lot cheaper.

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:37.020
If you have AI money, you can
go and buy these companies;

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:40.380
and that's why you can
own the distributions.

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:44.790
So, distribution, in a way, can
be acquired with more money.

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:47.580
Another I thought
about is expertise.

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:49.200
So, people were like, okay.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:52.170
But I know the domain
the best, right?

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:56.010
I have years and years of
iterations and make it the best.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:12:00.510
But expertise is interesting
because expertise,

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:03.300
for it to be useful, you have
to encode it somewhere, right?

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:04.890
You don't want to
be a service model

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:08.640
when you can just use exchange
service for hourly money, right,

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:09.810
talk to people or not.

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:11.168
So, you want to
just scale it, so

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:12.510
that means that you encode that.

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:14.280
And now, expertise
becomes software.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:17.310
And all the workflows, if you
spend years and years designing,

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:23.400
can be just copied; and people
can just use that as a workflow.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:25.650
So, I think exactly one thing
that I realized is just

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:29.310
like, what part is momentum, so
the only way to spend ahead

00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:31.680
of customers, like
your competitors,

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:33.690
is just to move
faster than them.

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:38.010
And I think it makes a lot of
pressure for a lot of companies,

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:39.870
because now, you want
to build product.

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.510
You're committed to a
never-ending treadmill

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:46.380
of just doing faster and
faster than the competitors,

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:48.390
which is for a lot of
people it's great.

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:49.260
People love it.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:57.420
But it just creates a lot of
pressure on the part of how

00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:00.480
to stay with the competitors.

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:01.620
How do you feel so far?

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:03.640
Is this too bleak?

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:10.200
So, here's a question
of what to build.

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:12.660
I think it's a lot of form.

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:14.490
I do think it's like a
lot of value in building,

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:17.940
because building, one thing is,
I do spend a lot of time.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:20.880
Right now, I have both Claude
Code and Codex running,

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:22.920
because I feel like it's
just like building.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:25.140
And building things, I feel

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:27.420
like it makes me a better
problem solver, right?

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:28.950
It's because, when
you're building,

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:31.740
you don't build products that
exist in a vacuum, right?

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:35.370
You build something to address
some annoyances that you have.

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:36.964
So, building just, like, helps

00:13:36.976 --> 00:13:38.850
me understand
the problems I want

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:41.250
to solve more and have
the experience of, okay.

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:43.530
Here's a problem; here's a
solution I think would work.

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:45.510
Let's build it and launch it.

00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:46.830
And I put it in front of users,

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:49.020
and I see what
they say about it.

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:52.950
But, if you want to build it
as a company, as a startup,

00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:55.200
then you need to think
about not just like today,

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:59.280
but how it's going to work two
years from now on because,

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:02.550
under the case, I actually
invested in this company.

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:05.550
And they did great for
the first three years.

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:06.510
My first three years,

00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:09.510
I grew from my zero ARRs
and a 10 million ARR.

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:12.210
And now they just
don't know, okay.

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:13.320
Now we have 10 million ARR,

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:15.540
but then they cannot
get to 100 million ARR.

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:18.660
And in a lot of VC world,
it's got stuck, right?

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:20.640
You're not going to be
big enough to go IPO.

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:23.580
But then, what do you do next?

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:26.460
So, yeah. So, anyway,
it's very easy to keep

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:28.020
on building, CLE momentum.

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:30.180
And then don't know, if
you don't have a strategy

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:34.260
of going big, it's
hard to raise money.

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:37.620
So, one thing is, I do
have this belief that,

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:39.553
no matter how good AI is, there

00:14:39.565 --> 00:14:41.760
will always be
problems to solve,

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:44.130
because I think it's like,
no matter how good AI is,

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:47.280
I would never stop being angry
at people on the internet.

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:48.750
And I would never
stop being angry

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:51.300
at like, united customer
service, right?

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:53.130
There's a lot of
problems that it's just

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:55.140
like, AI will not solve.

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:57.690
And I do think it's like,
as AI becomes better,

00:14:57.690 --> 00:14:59.820
it expands the
surface areas, right.

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:05.610
As AI allow us to build more
use cases, as I expand,

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:08.130
it might not be the same
problems that we had before;

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:09.780
but I introduce new problems.

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:12.480
And I want to cover a few
categories of problems,

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:15.720
of things that have
emerged with AI.

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:16.830
So, before I go into that,

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:20.070
I think a major problem
is a long-tail problem.

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:22.200
It's a long-tail distribution.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:25.650
So, there are problems
everyone faces, right?

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:27.720
Everyone wants to
write better emails.

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:29.790
A lot of people want
to write better code.

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:33.030
A lot of people want to help

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:35.010
with small little
things like, okay.

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:36.210
Fix my essay.

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:37.380
Do my homework.

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:40.980
So, they're like a category
of very, very big problems

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:44.130
that I do believe that a lot
of AI frontier labs will try

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:46.260
to get the models to
do well on, right.

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:46.980
Now, let's look at them.

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:50.160
Okay. Here are the problems
that face maybe 99 percent

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.830
as a population, versus the
problems that face maybe,

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:54.630
like, 1 percent of
the population.

00:15:54.630 --> 00:15:57.000
Then AI frontier lab will
probably focus on the ones

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:59.310
of like 99 percent
a population has.

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:03.520
So, that's one reason why a lot
of this AI lab still focuses

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:06.630
on a few standard use cases,

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:08.670
and a lot of them still
focus on English.

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:11.610
For many, many languages,
for example, Arabic,

00:16:11.610 --> 00:16:14.130
AI actually doesn't
quite work well.

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:15.420
Doesn't work quite well.

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:18.540
So, I have a friend
who is Iranian.

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:19.830
And she told me this.

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:25.140
She's a professor at CMU, so
she does a lot of work with AI.

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:29.470
And she tells a story,
because she speaks Farsi,

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:31.740
is that the language,
Farsi, right?

00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:34.470
Sorry. I feel very
stupid right now.

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:35.430
Yeah, Farsi.

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:39.480
And she has an issue of
her friends would ask her

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:42.810
to explain a sentence,
like an idiom in Farsi.

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:45.810
And she explains a sentence
that, oh, that means like that.

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:46.680
And that friend was like, No.

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:50.430
You're lying, because apparently
ChatGPT gives a different answer

00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:53.580
to that different answer.

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:56.340
And so, friends somehow
trust ChatGPT more.

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:57.510
Even my friend's like, "No, no.

00:16:57.510 --> 00:16:58.470
I am correct."

00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:03.120
But just because ChatGPT doesn't
do very well in Farsi language.

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:05.730
So, yeah. So they
work with some,

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:07.710
a lot of the voice chatbot.

00:17:07.710 --> 00:17:10.590
And another problem with voice
chatbot is that you have

00:17:10.590 --> 00:17:12.690
to respond very fast, right.

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:16.230
Like a human, human to human
interactions, so latency is

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:19.350
like you want it to be under
like 800 milliseconds for it

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:24.210
to be like good, reasonable,
natural conversations.

00:17:24.210 --> 00:17:27.840
But, for voice chatbot, it has
to go through three steps.

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:30.090
So, first step it should
be able to translate

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:32.700
from audio into text, right?

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:35.790
And then, you send the text to
an LM to get back to response.

00:17:35.790 --> 00:17:40.440
And then you send the response
to what I call text-to-speech

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:43.950
to synthesize the voice
to send it back to users.

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:48.630
So, a challenge we have was that
this voice chat work really well

00:17:48.630 --> 00:17:50.100
for English, because the speed

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:52.740
of synthesizing English
is pretty fast.

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:55.410
But, for Arabic or
Russian, it's extremely slow.

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:57.600
So, it's like it's really,

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:00.240
really hard to create
natural voice chatbots

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:01.950
for different languages.

00:18:01.950 --> 00:18:04.950
So, yeah. So I think I see for
a lot of long-tail problems,

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:08.670
I do see the AI labs
as not quite doing it.

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:10.740
And it doesn't mean that
they're not doing it

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:12.330
like two years from now on.

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:16.050
But I can see that, if you
can pick a problem, that is,

00:18:16.050 --> 00:18:21.030
I think of them as big enough to
be profitable but not big enough

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:22.560
that AI frontier
labs wouldn't want

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:25.290
to take the launch,
that kind of thing.

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:26.640
Okay. So, here's some

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:29.280
of the long-tail problems
I could find, I thought

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:30.440
was interesting.

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:32.750
One is human preferences.

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:35.540
Are you here familiar with RLHF?

00:18:35.540 --> 00:18:38.510
Have you heard of the term
reinforcement learning

00:18:38.510 --> 00:18:41.180
by human preference?

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:42.260
You heard of the term.

00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:49.430
So, yeah. So, in the early days
of LM, in the early day of GPT-4

00:18:49.430 --> 00:18:54.620
and as early day of these
models, RLHF is one of the most,

00:18:54.620 --> 00:18:58.740
I think, most popular terms
that people want to understand.

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:01.320
So, the way the model trained,
they trained the model is

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:05.400
that they want the AI to
go to generate responses

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:08.630
that humans prefer, right?

00:19:08.630 --> 00:19:10.520
So, it wouldn't give a prompt,

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:13.350
and it gave two
responses side by side.

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:14.490
And then the human would pick.

00:19:14.490 --> 00:19:16.500
Okay; this is the one
that I like more.

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:18.870
And then you would use
that to train the model,

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:22.860
to nudge it toward sharing
more better responses.

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:25.140
So, a challenge with that
is that it's trying

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:29.760
to encode human preference into
one equation, so it assumes

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:32.720
that there's some like
universal preference, right,

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:35.150
like everyone somehow
prefers the same thing.

00:19:35.150 --> 00:19:38.120
So, when I was going through the
Anthropic RLHF datasets they

00:19:38.120 --> 00:19:40.700
made online, I realized a lot

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:43.910
of what people consider the
winning responses, actually I do

00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:45.200
not prefer the winning response.

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:47.570
I actually prefer the
losing responses,

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:52.490
just like somehow the note
is like, smart is not better.

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:55.200
So, human preferences
are extremely hard,

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:58.140
and it requires you
understand users very well.

00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:01.560
So, going back to the
voice chatbot, right,

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:05.430
so humans actually have a very
different preference of how fast

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:06.960
the other person responds.

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:08.340
So, I'm from Vietnam.

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:12.930
So, in Vietnam, people actually
prefer the other person

00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:15.060
to give them time
before a response.

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:18.240
So, I have a niece
who is 10 years old,

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:21.930
and I have my godmother, who's
a 70 year-old American woman.

00:20:21.930 --> 00:20:23.730
So, I tried to put
them on a call,

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:26.580
and it was the worst experience.

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:28.590
So, the thing, so
when my godmother,

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:29.970
she tried to be friendly, right.

00:20:29.970 --> 00:20:32.580
So, when my niece
took time to respond,

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:34.290
my godmother was like, okay.

00:20:34.290 --> 00:20:35.280
She is shy.

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:36.300
She doesn't know how to talk,

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:38.040
and she didn't want
the awkward silence.

00:20:38.040 --> 00:20:40.500
So, she kept, if my niece
wait a little bit long

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:43.520
and didn't say anything,
my godmother kept

00:20:43.520 --> 00:20:44.990
on asking questions, right?

00:20:44.990 --> 00:20:46.850
And then, after that, my niece
told me, it's like, wow.

00:20:46.850 --> 00:20:49.910
She didn't give me any
time to respond at all.

00:20:49.910 --> 00:20:51.954
So, it turns out,
there's actually

00:20:51.966 --> 00:20:53.670
a well-known study about it.

00:20:53.670 --> 00:20:55.110
It's about, in the West,

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:57.990
people actually prefer
conversation respond rates to be

00:20:57.990 --> 00:21:00.510
very fast, maybe
18 milliseconds.

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:04.470
If you finish talking, you
expect me to respond very,

00:21:04.470 --> 00:21:08.550
very fast, whereas in some
cultures, like Vietnam,

00:21:08.550 --> 00:21:13.020
after you finish talking,
you give the person maybe

00:21:13.020 --> 00:21:17.910
like 200 milliseconds so that
the other person can make sure

00:21:17.910 --> 00:21:20.940
the other person has finished
and formulates a response.

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:22.920
So, all of these
nuances you need to go

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:25.890
to understand the customers
to build a product

00:21:25.890 --> 00:21:28.620
that works quite well.

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:33.060
So, I guess part of the
expertise as part of the moat,

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:35.670
it's more like user
understanding, understanding

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:37.560
who you're building for.

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:40.560
Another thing is, like, the
human-AI interactions.

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:44.190
And, since we're at Microsoft
Build, I want to talk

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:47.700
about a lot of you are,
like, software engineers.

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:51.480
How many of you are using AI
to write code right now?

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:52.290
Almost all of you.

00:21:52.290 --> 00:21:56.940
Yeah. How many of you are
using it via the Terminal?

00:21:56.940 --> 00:21:57.390
Some of you.

00:21:57.390 --> 00:22:01.180
Right. How many of
you use it via ID?

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:04.440
How many use both?

00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:06.690
Okay. Well, most of
you use both.

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:07.740
So, when I was looking,

00:22:07.740 --> 00:22:10.560
it's like Terminal is a
very interesting product.

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:13.960
So, Terminal, I think of it
as a more legacy product.

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:19.110
Do you think that Terminal is
easy to use or hard to use?

00:22:19.110 --> 00:22:21.870
Who thinks terminals
are easy to use?

00:22:21.870 --> 00:22:22.740
One person, right.

00:22:22.740 --> 00:22:23.640
Few people.

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:27.540
So, Terminal, when I started
using Terminal for writing code,

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:29.340
it got pretty annoying, right?

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:32.010
It's very hard to move
the cursor around.

00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:34.650
You kind of just throw
an image in there.

00:22:34.650 --> 00:22:36.660
If you see a user-built
website, it's like, okay.

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:38.640
This is, like, a
horrible interface.

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:40.500
I want AI to see that
this is terrible,

00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:42.810
but I cannot easily
drop an image in there.

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:46.620
And it's made me think, why
are terminals so hard to use?

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:47.850
And it made me think of, okay.

00:22:47.850 --> 00:22:49.500
Terminals are actually
very powerful.

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:52.710
Terminals are like a control
plane for the computer, right?

00:22:52.710 --> 00:22:54.900
It's very easy to do
stupid things there.

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:56.280
You can remove RF.

00:22:56.280 --> 00:22:57.810
They can remove the
whole operating system,

00:22:57.810 --> 00:22:59.250
and you could be,
like, in trouble.

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:02.670
So, terminals, in a way, was
designed to be hard-to-use

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:04.680
on purpose, so that people

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:07.470
who don't know what they are
doing don't do something stupid

00:23:07.470 --> 00:23:08.850
by accident, right.

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:10.470
So, IDE, on the other hand,

00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:12.900
just makes it
a lot easier to use.

00:23:12.900 --> 00:23:14.280
But then I thought about it.

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:17.670
It's like, there's not really
a reason why they need to be

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:19.380
in a separate product, right?

00:23:19.380 --> 00:23:22.020
One of them is, like, ease of
use, and the other makes it,

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:23.460
like, harder to control.

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:27.240
So, I think, what if we have
something just like as a hybrid.

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:29.790
And I think it's like a lot
of those Codex desktops,

00:23:29.790 --> 00:23:33.000
or like Claude Code desktop,
or like a different desktop,

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:34.800
design should be a
mixture of it, right.

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:37.140
It has an ease of
use of like IDE,

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:40.380
but then you also allow each
access of file systems,

00:23:40.380 --> 00:23:42.360
the ways that a terminal can,

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:45.120
so that it may give you
that more power.

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:47.640
And another thing is about it,
people have quickly realized

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:54.510
that which was so good we
quickly realize this is like,

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:56.820
have you ever walked around
with your computer open

00:23:56.820 --> 00:24:01.200
because of Claude Code or Codex
is running on the computer?

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:02.850
Yeah. I think I walk
around the airport.

00:24:02.850 --> 00:24:04.800
And, when I see somebody
walking around open, okay;

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:07.170
that person has
something running.

00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:10.080
So, there's no reason why
you need to do that, right,

00:24:10.080 --> 00:24:12.480
because I even think about
all the work is being done

00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:13.410
on the cloud.

00:24:13.410 --> 00:24:14.730
There's no reason why you need

00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:17.130
to keep the computer
open to run it.

00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:20.730
But, then, so there's a lot of
tools to make it easy for you

00:24:20.730 --> 00:24:25.560
to access, to make your things
run without the computer.

00:24:25.560 --> 00:24:28.230
So, somebody likes to
open Claude early in the day.

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:30.840
A lot of people bought the
MacBook Mini, so the idea is

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:33.750
that you have a server
that hosts your agent

00:24:33.750 --> 00:24:35.430
so that you can just leave
it running all the time;

00:24:35.430 --> 00:24:37.650
so you don't need
to use the computer.

00:24:37.650 --> 00:24:40.470
But after that it creates a
whole challenge of like, okay.

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:41.910
You need to create a sandbox.

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:45.030
You need to copy to
recreate the environment.

00:24:45.030 --> 00:24:47.040
So, now it has to be on the
cloud so you can access

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:48.630
from a different device,
and there are some people

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:51.690
who create mobile apps so you
can control the Claude Code

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:53.790
or Codex from the mobile phone.

00:24:53.790 --> 00:24:55.170
Actually, one of my
fun projects was

00:24:55.170 --> 00:24:57.360
like, I can actually
control my Claude Code

00:24:57.360 --> 00:24:59.700
from my Telegram bot.

00:24:59.700 --> 00:25:00.990
So, it could send me messages.

00:25:00.990 --> 00:25:02.250
Okay. Now it's done;
they ask me yes,

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:03.390
no questions, they can, okay.

00:25:03.390 --> 00:25:04.500
Do it. Do it.

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:07.290
So, I think all of that
human-AI interaction is

00:25:07.290 --> 00:25:09.210
quite interesting.

00:25:09.210 --> 00:25:13.590
Another challenge, another
example with GitHub,

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:17.550
so, I imagine a lot of
you probably use GitHub

00:25:17.550 --> 00:25:20.580
for code review,
collaborations, right?

00:25:20.580 --> 00:25:22.980
And one thing I noticed
with code review is

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:26.340
that people don't review
code the old way anymore.

00:25:26.340 --> 00:25:28.230
So, I worked with a
team recently,

00:25:28.230 --> 00:25:32.010
and the most senior person
told me that he still reviews

00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:35.640
on the code line by line,
right, which is shocking to me

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:37.830
because it sounds so
painful nowadays.

00:25:37.830 --> 00:25:39.870
I don't read code line
by line anymore, right.

00:25:39.870 --> 00:25:41.340
This is too painful.

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:44.370
But he said, oh, he did it
because he wants to be able

00:25:44.370 --> 00:25:46.950
to give feedback to
junior engineers

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:48.000
so that they can learn.

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:49.590
Okay. This is not
good or not bad.

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:51.000
And then it went to
junior engineers.

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:51.600
And, okay.

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:54.060
Do you read his reviews?

00:25:54.060 --> 00:25:55.830
And they were like, no.

00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:57.240
And the reason he's
told me, they told me

00:25:57.240 --> 00:26:01.470
that because the feedback
is not actionable,

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:03.390
because the senior
engineer will say, "Okay,

00:26:03.390 --> 00:26:06.150
do not write this if-else
function like this.

00:26:06.150 --> 00:26:07.350
Write it like that."

00:26:07.350 --> 00:26:09.720
But then those junior
engineers are not the people

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:11.670
who write the code, right?

00:26:11.670 --> 00:26:14.220
It can tell AI, "Okay,

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:15.300
don't write this thing."

00:26:15.300 --> 00:26:17.940
But it's not going to be
applicable for the future.

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:21.240
So, what the junior engineers
want feedback on is how

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:26.010
to write the instructions
for the code.

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:28.380
And that's something I feel like
GitHub is not quite designed

00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:32.910
for right now, right, because I
think, with the new AI coding,

00:26:32.910 --> 00:26:34.410
the artifact is no
longer the code.

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:37.620
Like GitHub was designed around
code as a main artifact, right?

00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:39.600
You have the codebase.

00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:43.980
People protect the codebase.

00:26:43.980 --> 00:26:45.840
So, the code is the
main artifact.

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:48.810
But nowadays with AI coding,
the main artifact action is

00:26:48.810 --> 00:26:50.160
the instructions.

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:52.110
How do you like the specs?

00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:54.690
I think several coding
spec-driven development is

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:58.380
like how do you, if you have
a good spec, you can give it

00:26:58.380 --> 00:27:00.990
to AI; it can generate
code, maybe even better

00:27:00.990 --> 00:27:03.510
than what you currently have.

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:05.370
So, yeah. So, I think it's
a lot of work thinking

00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:06.390
about designing, okay.

00:27:06.390 --> 00:27:09.600
What would be the workflow
for us to work with AI

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:12.390
so that it can be more
meaningful, because a lot

00:27:12.390 --> 00:27:16.170
of the workflows that we
have nowadays are legacy.

00:27:16.170 --> 00:27:18.600
So, I guess it's like also part
of, like, human collaborations.

00:27:18.600 --> 00:27:21.270
It's like, how do we get
different people to work

00:27:21.270 --> 00:27:26.490
on a codebase effectively
with AI in the loop as well.

00:27:26.490 --> 00:27:28.140
And another category of tools,

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:32.940
of problems I find
exciting, is the human-AI

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:34.560
environment interactions.

00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:38.700
So, to make AI work better in
production, right, one way to go

00:27:38.700 --> 00:27:42.240
is to make AI better and
do things better,

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:44.268
another way to
go is also to make

00:27:44.280 --> 00:27:46.080
the world an easy environment

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:47.970
for AI to operate in.

00:27:47.970 --> 00:27:51.060
So, one thing with AI coding is

00:27:51.060 --> 00:27:53.730
that I have noticed
that AI works a lot better

00:27:53.730 --> 00:27:54.870
with new codebases.

00:27:54.870 --> 00:27:58.560
Right. Now, you have a new
codebase; AI can just do things.

00:27:58.560 --> 00:28:02.580
It also works a lot better
with modular codebase.

00:28:02.580 --> 00:28:03.780
Okay. It's like, okay.

00:28:03.780 --> 00:28:05.130
Just build these new functions.

00:28:05.130 --> 00:28:07.890
It's a lot easier than trying
to fix the old codebase.

00:28:07.890 --> 00:28:12.900
So, there's a whole new category
of consulting firms that go

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:15.840
and retrofit the big
existing codebase

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:20.580
to make them more friendly to AI
coding, so make it more modular

00:28:20.580 --> 00:28:23.640
with different separate
and massive monorepos

00:28:23.640 --> 00:28:26.910
into smaller repos
and things like that.

00:28:26.910 --> 00:28:30.060
And another example
is also API.

00:28:30.060 --> 00:28:32.400
So, in the past, a
lot of the tools

00:28:32.400 --> 00:28:34.320
and apps were built for humans.

00:28:34.320 --> 00:28:37.980
And what humans want are
all the GUI, right,

00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:40.170
like the graphic user
interface because,

00:28:40.170 --> 00:28:41.670
humans, we are very visual.

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:43.830
We want to be able to,
if we go to Salesforce,

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:46.200
we want to open the
website; we go and see,

00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:47.910
and here's a button
we want to click.

00:28:47.910 --> 00:28:49.470
And we want the
color to look nice.

00:28:49.470 --> 00:28:50.760
We want things to represent it.

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:54.570
But AI actually doesn't do
very well on the visual.

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:57.450
Right. Now, if you ask AI to
do Salesforce, you really want

00:28:57.450 --> 00:29:00.720
to code a bunch of API
functions, so we see that a lot

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:05.220
of companies try to move
a lot more of their design

00:29:05.220 --> 00:29:09.840
into API design, instead
of the GUI design.

00:29:09.840 --> 00:29:13.350
And I think it's very clear to
see the usage of, first of all,

00:29:13.350 --> 00:29:16.200
let's say you have a
tool like Salesforce.

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:17.910
You can see there's a
usage on the Salesforce,

00:29:17.910 --> 00:29:19.650
how much of that
was used by human

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:21.240
and how much is used by AI.

00:29:21.240 --> 00:29:25.170
You can see the distribution
changes a lot over time.

00:29:25.170 --> 00:29:27.120
Another thing, more
physical world.

00:29:27.120 --> 00:29:29.370
So, I work a lot with
robotics nowadays.

00:29:29.370 --> 00:29:33.780
And a lot of the
arguments people have

00:29:33.780 --> 00:29:37.380
against general purpose robots
is, like, why do we need a robot

00:29:37.380 --> 00:29:39.480
that can do everything and
work everywhere instead

00:29:39.480 --> 00:29:43.080
of just change my house or
change the road to be better,

00:29:43.080 --> 00:29:44.850
easier for robots, right?

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:47.910
So, one example is a
food delivery robot.

00:29:47.910 --> 00:29:49.285
Do you know these little food

00:29:49.297 --> 00:29:50.970
delivery robots
that move around?

00:29:50.970 --> 00:29:53.190
I think in the South
you see them a lot.

00:29:53.190 --> 00:29:53.910
They're very cute.

00:29:53.910 --> 00:29:54.630
They're very tiny.

00:29:54.630 --> 00:29:57.510
They show up like this, so that
you can put the food inside,

00:29:57.510 --> 00:29:59.280
and it takes it to
your home, and you open it.

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:01.440
And it's adorable.

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:03.000
So, I was talking to the team.

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:06.120
I was asking, so what is
a weird challenge you had

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:07.170
with this robot?

00:30:07.170 --> 00:30:08.130
And they were like, oh.

00:30:08.130 --> 00:30:11.610
The biggest challenge was the
robot couldn't cross the street.

00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:13.518
So, when the robot
would go in the

00:30:13.530 --> 00:30:15.450
street and the
light turned green,

00:30:15.450 --> 00:30:17.880
and a lot of streetlights
need you to press a button

00:30:17.880 --> 00:30:20.190
so that the light
can turn green.

00:30:20.190 --> 00:30:22.440
But the robots do not have arms.

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:26.100
It's very tiny, so it
cannot press a button

00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:27.570
to cross the street.

00:30:27.570 --> 00:30:29.790
So, somebody has an
interesting innovation of,

00:30:29.790 --> 00:30:31.950
if the robots can go
up to a pedestrian

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:33.330
and ask the person, "Hey.

00:30:33.330 --> 00:30:35.310
Can you press
the button for me?"

00:30:35.310 --> 00:30:37.410
There was some video online
and the pedestrian was like,

00:30:37.410 --> 00:30:39.570
"What is happening, is
this robot asking me

00:30:39.570 --> 00:30:41.130
to do manual labor for it?

00:30:41.130 --> 00:30:42.480
I should be asking
you to do things,

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:45.000
not the other way around."

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:45.390
But, yeah.

00:30:45.390 --> 00:30:48.090
So, he told me it's like
actually some cities,

00:30:48.090 --> 00:30:49.470
they have this kind of thing.

00:30:49.470 --> 00:30:51.900
It's like they call it
a streetlight API,

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:54.420
so that all this robotics
company can actually access the

00:30:54.420 --> 00:30:56.220
city streetlight,
and they can tell it

00:30:56.220 --> 00:30:57.750
when the light's
going to turn green.

00:30:57.750 --> 00:30:59.790
And, instead of pressing
the button for the light

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:01.980
to turn green, you can
actually call the API

00:31:01.980 --> 00:31:03.210
to turn the light green.

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:05.670
So, that's one example
of getting the world

00:31:05.670 --> 00:31:08.580
to be more AI-
friendly long-term.

00:31:08.580 --> 00:31:10.320
I think this is the
whole feel of it.

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:13.200
I think that we are
still very early,

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:16.260
because we still don't quite
know what use cases are going

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:20.320
to be possible and how we can
make the world better for it.

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:24.900
Another thing is, it's
pretty important for the robot

00:31:24.900 --> 00:31:27.000
to operate in the real world.

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:29.760
It's kind of like
a revisability.

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:33.840
So, recently Claude Code
actually deleted my database.

00:31:33.840 --> 00:31:38.640
So, I asked it to do an app, and
I want you deploy on a port.

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:42.240
It's like a Docker
Postgres port.

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:45.180
And I have another app
already using that port.

00:31:45.180 --> 00:31:47.790
So, Claude was like, I saw
that the port is taken.

00:31:47.790 --> 00:31:50.670
Let me clear it so that
we could deploy this app;

00:31:50.670 --> 00:31:54.000
and then it completely deleted
the other Postgres database.

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:55.500
Wow. Smart.

00:31:55.500 --> 00:31:58.230
So, I was mighty amused.

00:31:58.230 --> 00:32:03.210
But was not frustrated because
I have a backup, right?

00:32:03.210 --> 00:32:04.380
But it could happen, right?

00:32:04.380 --> 00:32:08.010
So, for a lot of things that
happens in on your computer,

00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:11.370
there's a way for you
to revert what happens.

00:32:11.370 --> 00:32:14.370
Git is great for it;
if you submit a PR

00:32:14.370 --> 00:32:16.050
or, like, make a commit
you don't like,

00:32:16.050 --> 00:32:17.910
or it reverses the old commit.

00:32:17.910 --> 00:32:21.360
But, for a lot of use cases,
you don't have that luxury.

00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:25.020
So, let's say you ask an AI
agent to submit a form, right.

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:28.230
After you submit the form, maybe
the form, the data now lives

00:32:28.230 --> 00:32:29.700
in somebody else's server.

00:32:29.700 --> 00:32:32.970
You cannot undo it unless
you ask the server,

00:32:32.970 --> 00:32:35.250
the form owner, to
delete it for you.

00:32:35.250 --> 00:32:36.540
So, you don't have that luxury.

00:32:36.540 --> 00:32:39.540
You cannot just transfer
money to another bank account

00:32:39.540 --> 00:32:41.940
and then ask a bank account
to transfer it back.

00:32:41.940 --> 00:32:42.690
They might.

00:32:42.690 --> 00:32:45.870
But, if they are like the
scammers, if we want, right?

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:50.220
So, there are a lot of things
that, as we give AI more power,

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:55.380
more tools, it's just
the risk is just higher.

00:32:55.380 --> 00:32:58.020
So, we need to design the
whole system around how

00:32:58.020 --> 00:33:01.860
to make it very, very hard
for AI not to make mistakes

00:33:01.860 --> 00:33:04.920
because mistakes can be very
costly and not reversible

00:33:04.920 --> 00:33:06.870
for many, many use cases.

00:33:06.870 --> 00:33:09.660
And especially working robotics,
it's important, more important

00:33:09.660 --> 00:33:12.960
than ever because, if a
robot step on a child,

00:33:12.960 --> 00:33:14.970
I don't think we can
just revert that, right.

00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:16.710
The child's really hurt.

00:33:16.710 --> 00:33:19.530
So, actually one of the
robotic companies I work

00:33:19.530 --> 00:33:20.970
with, because they
want to use cases,

00:33:20.970 --> 00:33:24.960
we say that they get asked for
a lot is elderly care, right?

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:29.400
I'm not sure any one of you
have to deal with elderly care.

00:33:29.400 --> 00:33:30.870
It's extremely expensive.

00:33:30.870 --> 00:33:33.480
Some people pay $20,000 a month

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:35.970
to take care of an
elderly parent.

00:33:35.970 --> 00:33:39.570
So, the need
for it is very high.

00:33:39.570 --> 00:33:41.280
But elderly care has a thing.

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:44.550
It's scary, right,
because a robot is heavy.

00:33:44.550 --> 00:33:47.550
And robots has very
low battery life.

00:33:47.550 --> 00:33:52.170
So, for example, have you
seen the Unitree G1 robot?

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:53.700
It's like yay high, right?

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:55.650
So, it's like 80 pounds.

00:33:55.650 --> 00:33:57.090
So, it's metal, right.

00:33:57.090 --> 00:34:00.300
Imagine that falling on you.

00:34:00.300 --> 00:34:02.250
That does not feel good.

00:34:02.250 --> 00:34:04.440
And you can do a lot of
things to help with it,

00:34:04.440 --> 00:34:07.410
but one failure
is quite dumb.

00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:08.820
This battery just dies.

00:34:08.820 --> 00:34:11.340
So, let's imagine that the robot
is doing some walking, right,

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:12.990
and that the battery just dies.

00:34:12.990 --> 00:34:16.110
So, the robot, mid-action,
can just like flop over.

00:34:16.110 --> 00:34:17.490
So, it's quite challenging.

00:34:17.490 --> 00:34:19.320
So, we're doing things.

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:22.300
Oh. Do you know about
the one-wheel accident?

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:24.330
Do you know the one wheel?

00:34:24.330 --> 00:34:27.690
The one just has one wheel, and
they can go and ride around.

00:34:27.690 --> 00:34:30.270
It looks very dangerous
but very cool.

00:34:30.270 --> 00:34:34.050
So, they have one failure mode,
just like the Gotham suit a lot.

00:34:34.050 --> 00:34:38.250
It's just like, if you're on
the wheel and the battery died,

00:34:38.250 --> 00:34:40.110
you would just fly over there,

00:34:40.110 --> 00:34:41.880
because the wheel
is stuck there.

00:34:41.880 --> 00:34:43.530
So, that could
happen with robots.

00:34:43.530 --> 00:34:45.360
Let's say it's like
the robots do a lot

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:46.800
of actions, and
the battery dies.

00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:49.730
It would just have no
control over itself.

00:34:49.730 --> 00:34:53.420
So, there are a lot of toolings,
work to make it safer.

00:34:53.420 --> 00:34:57.440
Maybe detect, once a
battery is maybe low,

00:34:57.440 --> 00:35:00.180
then you don't attempt
any complicated actions.

00:35:00.180 --> 00:35:03.050
Or self-charging is a
very important feature

00:35:03.050 --> 00:35:05.390
so that we develop, so that
even though it's robotic, okay.

00:35:05.390 --> 00:35:06.958
If we're going to run out of

00:35:06.970 --> 00:35:09.170
battery, let's move
back to the base.

00:35:09.170 --> 00:35:13.130
And also, there are the rules
that we can do around safety.

00:35:13.130 --> 00:35:15.210
How much time do we have left?

00:35:16.530 --> 00:35:18.000
Yeah. Yeah.

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:19.950
So, I think there's
a lot of problems

00:35:19.950 --> 00:35:23.040
that we can build
for AI, with AI.

00:35:23.040 --> 00:35:25.980
I'm actually pretty excited
because one thing I realized is,

00:35:25.980 --> 00:35:27.781
with AI, I get to build more

00:35:27.793 --> 00:35:30.060
things, because
now I do not have

00:35:30.060 --> 00:35:31.650
to do a lot of boring stuff.

00:35:31.650 --> 00:35:33.480
I can just focus on
thinking about, okay.

00:35:33.480 --> 00:35:35.970
What kind of new things, new
problems I want to solve.

00:35:35.970 --> 00:35:39.690
And a lot of this boring stuff,
to be fair, I actually do not

00:35:39.690 --> 00:35:43.290
like manually writing
code because, in college,

00:35:43.290 --> 00:35:46.770
one thing I really hated
was vectorizations, right?

00:35:46.770 --> 00:35:47.910
I had to do something,
it's, okay.

00:35:47.910 --> 00:35:51.180
You have to make it super fast,
or writing if-else functions.

00:35:51.180 --> 00:35:53.100
I never found that
very, very exciting.

00:35:53.100 --> 00:35:54.930
But now, with AI,
upload a play code.

00:35:54.930 --> 00:35:58.610
Now, with AI, you can
do that a lot faster.

00:35:58.610 --> 00:36:01.470
So, we talk about software
defensibility for the product,

00:36:01.470 --> 00:36:03.890
but another question that I
also think a lot about is,

00:36:03.890 --> 00:36:07.400
how to make myself more
defensible in terms of AI,

00:36:07.400 --> 00:36:12.510
right, because how do I make
sure that I can learn the skills

00:36:12.510 --> 00:36:14.490
that AI won't automate.

00:36:14.490 --> 00:36:16.348
So, in the early
days of ChatGPT,

00:36:16.360 --> 00:36:18.120
one thing that I did spend time

00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:21.870
on -- and I would love to learn
more from people how they do it,

00:36:21.870 --> 00:36:23.940
what I call like a career audit.

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:26.850
So, for the whole week, I
actually went through and wrote

00:36:26.850 --> 00:36:28.380
down everything that I did.

00:36:28.380 --> 00:36:30.270
And I tried to think of, okay.

00:36:30.270 --> 00:36:34.770
Can this thing be
automated by AI, right?

00:36:34.770 --> 00:36:37.560
If it can, is there
someone already doing it?

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:42.870
Or, if cannot be AI, or
if it does not exist yet,

00:36:42.870 --> 00:36:45.390
then maybe I can build it.

00:36:45.390 --> 00:36:47.430
So, they give me a lot
of ideas and so give me

00:36:47.430 --> 00:36:48.630
like a better understanding

00:36:48.630 --> 00:36:53.160
of so, build code is a
term of AI exposure.

00:36:53.160 --> 00:36:56.820
What percentage of your
job is exposed to AI?

00:36:56.820 --> 00:36:59.340
And, the higher the
percentage, the higher chance

00:36:59.340 --> 00:37:02.250
of the job being automated.

00:37:02.250 --> 00:37:03.780
So, yeah. I'm going
to be curious

00:37:03.780 --> 00:37:07.590
to see how people approach that.

00:37:07.590 --> 00:37:10.890
So I think, just to bring it
home, I think about any software

00:37:10.890 --> 00:37:13.380
that exists can be replicated.

00:37:13.380 --> 00:37:16.200
And so, I spent the
last two years

00:37:16.200 --> 00:37:18.390
in my last company to
think about, okay.

00:37:18.390 --> 00:37:23.310
If that's the case, maybe
should I go beyond software?

00:37:23.310 --> 00:37:26.370
So, I think of AI
agents as, an AI agent is

00:37:26.370 --> 00:37:28.890
about performing actions, right?

00:37:28.890 --> 00:37:31.950
In the earliest, in the
simplest form of action,

00:37:31.950 --> 00:37:34.380
it's just like token, like
generates the next token,

00:37:34.380 --> 00:37:37.560
which is the early days
of ChatGPT, LM, right?

00:37:37.560 --> 00:37:40.800
It's just keep on generating one
token after another.

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:44.370
Should I turn to an essay, an
email, question answering.

00:37:44.370 --> 00:37:46.680
And then the next category,

00:37:46.680 --> 00:37:49.770
next level of actions are
digital actions, right.

00:37:49.770 --> 00:37:52.680
Now, the agent can do fancy
things or can read files.

00:37:52.680 --> 00:37:55.500
It can write into files, which
is important for coding.

00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:56.760
It can code different APIs.

00:37:56.760 --> 00:37:59.130
From GitHub API, it can call,

00:37:59.130 --> 00:38:01.140
it can call image
generation API.

00:38:01.140 --> 00:38:02.760
It can call whatever,
the banking

00:38:02.760 --> 00:38:05.610
or internal API, external API.

00:38:05.610 --> 00:38:06.450
It can do search.

00:38:06.450 --> 00:38:09.300
Search is a very important
digital action.

00:38:09.300 --> 00:38:10.650
I think this is the next tier.

00:38:10.650 --> 00:38:13.140
I believe it's
physical actions.

00:38:13.140 --> 00:38:15.270
So, for example,
now, instead of --

00:38:15.270 --> 00:38:20.400
imagine, right, imagine that you
have an agent that's running

00:38:20.400 --> 00:38:22.170
some code, maybe
they open Claude

00:38:22.170 --> 00:38:23.700
or something on the terminal.

00:38:23.700 --> 00:38:25.620
And then suddenly it says, okay.

00:38:25.620 --> 00:38:28.980
For this to do complete
this task, maybe I need

00:38:28.980 --> 00:38:32.760
to build small things.

00:38:32.760 --> 00:38:34.650
It can run out and buy it.

00:38:34.650 --> 00:38:36.150
Or it can do, okay.

00:38:36.150 --> 00:38:39.210
I want to do this task,
like hosting an event.

00:38:39.210 --> 00:38:40.080
Maybe you can think, okay.

00:38:40.080 --> 00:38:42.210
For hosting event, I can
create a guest list.

00:38:42.210 --> 00:38:43.410
I can invite a guest.

00:38:43.410 --> 00:38:47.010
But I also need to go
out and buy some plates.

00:38:47.010 --> 00:38:51.030
If you can send, get some robot
to do these physical actions,

00:38:51.030 --> 00:38:52.230
that could be super cool.

00:38:52.230 --> 00:38:55.320
So, I do think there's a next
another layer of action

00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:57.660
in the real world, like
you do physical actions.

00:38:57.660 --> 00:38:58.470
And I do think of just

00:38:58.470 --> 00:39:00.565
like, physical AI
agents actually

00:39:00.577 --> 00:39:02.250
do not look that different

00:39:02.250 --> 00:39:05.070
from the AI agent
that we know today,

00:39:05.070 --> 00:39:07.590
because an agent is
anything that can interact

00:39:07.590 --> 00:39:09.630
with the environment,
right, take feedback

00:39:09.630 --> 00:39:12.210
from the environment and
perform actions on it.

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:15.770
So, a coding agent, the
environment here is

00:39:15.770 --> 00:39:18.080
like the computer, right.

00:39:18.080 --> 00:39:21.800
And the action you can do
is like refi, wifi, edit.

00:39:21.800 --> 00:39:23.480
So, it can perform actions.

00:39:23.480 --> 00:39:24.500
It can receive.

00:39:24.500 --> 00:39:26.090
It can perform actions
in the environment

00:39:26.090 --> 00:39:27.770
by making changes to the file.

00:39:27.770 --> 00:39:30.330
It can get feedback from
the environment first,

00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:32.820
where it can try to run the
file, try to see the compile

00:39:32.820 --> 00:39:36.020
to see if it passes a test.

00:39:36.020 --> 00:39:40.700
So, that's how anization work;
and it's the same thing

00:39:40.700 --> 00:39:42.649
with a physical
agent, but now the

00:39:42.661 --> 00:39:44.510
environment is physical, instead

00:39:44.510 --> 00:39:47.750
of just purely, purely digital.

00:39:47.750 --> 00:39:50.840
And the action here is more
physical motions, like walk,

00:39:50.840 --> 00:39:54.290
pick up things, placing
things, pushing, pulling.

00:39:54.290 --> 00:39:57.050
And AI, we have seen that
it has worked quite well

00:39:57.050 --> 00:39:58.310
with reasonings, right.

00:39:58.310 --> 00:40:02.550
With AI, the initial AI agent,
we can see, being given a task,

00:40:02.550 --> 00:40:04.680
it can reason through
step-by-step how

00:40:04.680 --> 00:40:06.510
to accomplish the task, right?

00:40:06.510 --> 00:40:08.310
Okay. If you want to do a
business app first,

00:40:08.310 --> 00:40:09.900
I need to create a database.

00:40:09.900 --> 00:40:11.130
I need to build as well.

00:40:11.130 --> 00:40:16.050
I need to write a script,
and things like that.

00:40:16.050 --> 00:40:19.470
And I have seen it; AI can
do the same thing for a lot

00:40:19.470 --> 00:40:20.880
of the tasks in the real world.

00:40:20.880 --> 00:40:23.280
Let's say if you ask
it, oh, do laundry,

00:40:23.280 --> 00:40:24.180
you can first think, okay.

00:40:24.180 --> 00:40:26.340
First, I need to collect
all the clothes.

00:40:26.340 --> 00:40:30.060
Second, I need to take them
to the washing machines.

00:40:30.060 --> 00:40:32.400
I can do a lot of random things.

00:40:32.400 --> 00:40:36.600
So, I think its reasoning
part is pretty good.

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:38.790
I do think I have pretty
strong convictions

00:40:38.790 --> 00:40:42.480
that AI is pretty
good at reasoning.

00:40:42.480 --> 00:40:46.470
AI needs to give it a physical
understanding because I need to,

00:40:46.470 --> 00:40:49.820
for digital AI, a
lot of the product,

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:52.400
a lot of environments
are very well-described

00:40:52.400 --> 00:40:53.600
with documentations.

00:40:53.600 --> 00:40:56.120
Not everything has
good documentations,

00:40:56.120 --> 00:40:57.890
but ideally, a lot of APIs.

00:40:57.890 --> 00:41:01.530
You know what endpoints
you can call, right.

00:41:01.530 --> 00:41:03.240
It knows what parameters.

00:41:03.240 --> 00:41:04.740
It expects, it knows, okay.

00:41:04.740 --> 00:41:05.610
Here's the error code.

00:41:05.610 --> 00:41:06.930
You know what that means.

00:41:06.930 --> 00:41:10.050
But, for a physical wall,
we don't quite have

00:41:10.050 --> 00:41:13.350
that nice description
of the wall.

00:41:13.350 --> 00:41:17.820
There's no documentation about
how to handle an egg, right?

00:41:17.820 --> 00:41:18.870
There's no one to say, Okay.

00:41:18.870 --> 00:41:22.140
If you put this much force
into the egg, it will break.

00:41:22.140 --> 00:41:26.220
Or like, if you put the leg
this way, you're going to fall.

00:41:26.220 --> 00:41:30.870
So, that's not good description
of the physical environment.

00:41:30.870 --> 00:41:33.240
So, that's the part that we
need to teach AI.

00:41:33.240 --> 00:41:36.630
We humans, we learn how to
operate in the real world

00:41:36.630 --> 00:41:37.950
to retry an error, right?

00:41:37.950 --> 00:41:41.670
For a try, it takes like a long
time, maybe a year or so just

00:41:41.670 --> 00:41:43.830
to learn just to
walk, like learning

00:41:43.830 --> 00:41:46.590
to control the
muscles, the joints.

00:41:46.590 --> 00:41:50.100
We don't have that
information encoded.

00:41:50.100 --> 00:41:52.470
And that's why there's
a lot of effort going

00:41:52.470 --> 00:41:54.180
to what is called
a world modeling,

00:41:54.180 --> 00:41:57.710
like trying to model the world,
so that we can teach AI how

00:41:57.710 --> 00:42:00.590
to understand and operate in it.

00:42:00.590 --> 00:42:00.890
But, yeah.

00:42:00.890 --> 00:42:02.690
So, I'm very excited about it.

00:42:02.690 --> 00:42:05.180
So, actually, one of the
companies that I work

00:42:05.180 --> 00:42:08.670
with have a demo
tomorrow for the robots.

00:42:08.670 --> 00:42:10.380
They have this dog robot.

00:42:10.380 --> 00:42:13.980
Unfortunately, they kind
of spare a humanoid.

00:42:13.980 --> 00:42:17.580
Humanoids are really
cool to watch.

00:42:17.580 --> 00:42:18.060
But, yeah.

00:42:18.060 --> 00:42:21.360
Unfortunately. I think the
dogs are also very cute.

00:42:21.360 --> 00:42:23.100
Yeah. If you want to
come, come for it.

00:42:23.100 --> 00:42:28.560
And, if you want to talk
about robots, get in touch.

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:32.010
I'm trying to write more about
software defensibility and AI

00:42:32.010 --> 00:42:33.630
for robotics in general.

00:42:33.630 --> 00:42:35.520
Yeah. Thank you so
